= Glossary

_Every term, abbreviation, and piece of shorthand in this guide, in plain words — from BFC to Ziggurat. Vanilla Beyond the Sword, patch 3.19._

== A

- *Aggressive*: Trait granting free Combat I to melee and gunpowder units, with faster barracks.
- *Agriculture*: Early technology unlocking farms, the foundation of food and growth.
- *Alphabet*: Technology unlocking tech trading and the Research build; in Beyond the Sword, spies too.
- *Anarchy*: Turns of no output when changing civics or state religion.
- *Apostolic Palace*: Beyond the Sword world project, unlocked by Theology, that lets followers of its religion vote on resolutions and on diplomatic victory.
- *Archer*: Early defender with bonuses in cities and on hills.
- *Archipelago*: Map type of many small islands where naval power rules.

== B

- *Beakers*: Research points; commerce becomes beakers through the science slider.
- *BFC*: Big Fat Cross — the 21 tiles a city can work.
- *Bronze Working*: Early technology unlocking the Slavery civic, axemen, and forest chopping.
- *Buddhism*: Religion founded by the first to research the Buddhism technology.
- *Bureaucracy*: Legal civic granting +50% commerce and +50% production in the capital.

== C

- *Capitulation*: Ending a losing war by accepting vassalage to the victor.
- *Caste System*: Labor civic allowing unlimited artist, merchant, and scientist specialists.
- *Charismatic*: Trait granting +1 happiness per city and cheaper promotions.
- *Civic*: A policy chosen from one of five categories: Government, Legal, Labor, Economy, and Religion.
- *Civil Service*: Technology unlocking Bureaucracy and letting farms chain irrigation.
- *Code of Laws*: Technology founding Confucianism and unlocking courthouses and Caste System.
- *Colony*: Releasing distant overseas cities as an autonomous state, friendly to its motherland.
- *Commerce*: City income, divided among research, culture, and gold.
- *Conquest*: Victory by capturing every rival's original capital.
- *Continents*: Map type with several large landmasses separated by ocean.
- *Corporation*: Economic institution founded by a great person, spread by executives, that consumes resources for bonuses.
- *Cottage*: Commerce improvement that grows through hamlet, village, and town stages.
- *Creative*: Trait granting +2 culture per city and faster libraries and theaters.
- *Cultural victory*: Victory by raising three cities to legendary culture.
- *Culture*: City output that expands borders, presses on neighbors, and feeds cultural victory.
- *Currency*: Technology adding +1 trade route per city and enabling Wealth.

== D

- *Deity*: The hardest difficulty level, where rivals enjoy large bonuses.
- *Diplomatic victory*: Winning a United Nations or Apostolic Palace vote.
- *Domination*: Victory by owning a large majority of the world's land and population.

== E

- *Emancipation*: Labor civic that doubles cottage growth and stirs unhappiness in empires that do not adopt it.
- *Environmentalism*: Economy civic granting +6 health, with forests, jungles, windmills, and forest preserves adding bonuses.
- *Espionage*: The Beyond the Sword spy system: points gathered to fund missions against rival civilizations.
- *Executive*: Unit that spreads a corporation to foreign cities.
- *Expansive*: Trait granting +2 health per city and faster granaries and harbors.

== F

- *Farm*: Improvement providing food, best beside fresh water.
- *Feudalism*: Technology unlocking longbows and the Vassalage civic.
- *Financial*: Trait granting +1 commerce on tiles with at least two commerce.
- *Food*: Yield that grows cities; food resources also add small health bonuses.
- *Forest preserve*: Beyond the Sword improvement that keeps forests for science and health.
- *Fort*: Improvement, unlocked by Mathematics, that defends like a city and connects resources.
- *Fractal*: Map type with organic, unpredictable coastlines.
- *Free Market*: Economy civic granting +1 trade route in every city.
- *Free Speech*: Legal civic granting +2 commerce from every town and +100% culture in all cities.

== G

- *Gandhi*: Indian leader with the Spiritual and Philosophical traits.
- *Golden Age*: Eight turns of +1 hammer and +1 commerce on worked tiles, double great person points, and no anarchy.
- *Great Artist*: Great person that adds culture to a city and can perform a culture bomb.
- *Great Engineer*: Great person that rushes production or boosts a city's output.
- *Great General*: Unit born from military victories that can attach to an army as an instructor or found a Military Academy.
- *Great Merchant*: Great person that runs a trade mission for gold or builds a Customs House.
- *Great Person Points*: Accumulated points that birth great people; each birth raises the next threshold.
- *Great Prophet*: Great person that builds a shrine in the holy city.
- *Great Scientist*: Great person that builds an Academy or discovers a technology.
- *Great Spy*: Great person that strengthens espionage or infiltrates a rival civilization.

== H

- *Hamlet*: The second growth stage of a cottage.
- *Hammers*: Production yield, spent to build units, buildings, and wonders.
- *Hereditary Rule*: Government civic granting +1 happiness per military unit stationed in a city.
- *Hinduism*: Religion founded by the first to research the Hinduism technology.
- *Holy city*: The city where a religion was founded, home of its shrine.

== I

- *Immortal*: Persian unique unit replacing the Chariot.
- *Imperialistic*: Trait granting faster settlers and more Great Generals.
- *Industrious*: Trait granting +50% wonder production and faster forges.
- *Iron*: Strategic resource that unlocks swordsmen and later military units.
- *Islam*: Religion founded with Divine Right.
- *Ivory*: Luxury resource granting happiness and enabling war elephants.

== J

- *Judaism*: Religion founded by the first to research Monotheism.
- *Jungle*: Dense vegetation blocking improvements until workers clear it.

== L

- *Liberalism*: Technology granting a free technology to its first discoverer.
- *Luxury resource*: Gold, silver, gems, fur, ivory, silk, spice, sugar, wine, incense, and dye — connected luxuries grant happiness.

== M

- *Marathon*: The longest game speed, 1500 turns.
- *Mass Media*: Technology unlocking the United Nations and broadcast towers.
- *Mathematics*: Technology unlocking catapults and forts, with better forest chopping.
- *Mercantilism*: Economy civic granting a free specialist per city at the cost of foreign trade routes.
- *Mine*: Improvement adding production, best on hills.
- *Mining*: Early technology unlocking mines for hills.
- *Monarch*: A high difficulty level, demanding solid play.

== N

- *Noble*: The default difficulty level, with no bonuses either way.
- *Normal*: The standard game speed, 500 turns.

== O

- *Obelisk*: Egyptian unique building replacing the Monument.
- *Oil*: Strategic resource powering modern units and buildings.
- *Open Borders*: Diplomatic agreement allowing free passage and foreign trade routes.
- *Organized*: Trait halving civic upkeep and speeding courthouses and factories.
- *Organized Religion*: Religion civic granting +25% building production in cities following your state religion.

== P

- *Pacifism*: Religion civic doubling great person points, at the cost of gold upkeep per military unit.
- *Pangaea*: Map type with one giant supercontinent.
- *Philosophical*: Trait doubling great person point production.
- *Philosophy*: Technology founding Taoism and unlocking Pacifism.
- *Plantation*: Improvement for luxury crops such as sugar, wine, and silk.
- *Praetorian*: Roman unique unit replacing the Swordsman.
- *Printing Press*: Technology adding commerce to villages and towns.
- *Protective*: Trait granting free defensive promotions to archery and gunpowder units.

== Q

- *Quick*: The fastest game speed, 250 turns.

== R

- *Railroad*: Improvement and technology speeding land movement and linking resources.
- *Random event*: Unexpected Beyond the Sword occurrence, from forest fires to merchant windfalls.
- *Redcoat*: English unique unit replacing the Rifleman.
- *Representation*: Government civic giving specialists +3 research and happiness in the largest cities.
- *Rifling*: Technology unlocking riflemen and cavalry.
- *Road*: Improvement speeding movement and connecting resources.

== S

- *Sabotage Production*: Espionage mission that destroys a rival's unfinished build.
- *Settler*: Unit that founds new cities.
- *Sid's Sushi Co.*: Corporation founded by a Great Merchant, turning seafood into food and culture.
- *Slavery*: Labor civic that lets cities sacrifice population to hurry production.
- *Space race*: Victory by completing the spaceship to Alpha Centauri first.
- *Specialists*: Citizens assigned roles such as scientist, merchant, or spy, producing yields and great person points.
- *Spiritual*: Trait allowing civic and religion changes without anarchy.
- *Spy*: Stealth unit that runs espionage missions.
- *Standard Ethanol*: Corporation founded by a Great Scientist, converting crops into production.
- *State Property*: Economy civic removing distance maintenance and disabling corporations, with +10% production everywhere.
- *State religion*: The faith an empire officially follows, boosting cities that share it.
- *Strategic resource*: Iron, copper, horses, stone, marble, coal, oil, uranium, and aluminum — resources needed for key units and buildings.

== T

- *Taoism*: Religion founded by the first to research Philosophy.
- *Theocracy*: Religion civic granting new units +2 experience while blocking other faiths from spreading.
- *Theology*: Technology founding Christianity and unlocking the Apostolic Palace.
- *Time victory*: If no one wins by 2050, the highest score takes the game.
- *Town*: The final cottage stage, richest in commerce.
- *Traits*: Leader characteristics granting permanent empire-wide bonuses.

== U

- *Unique building*: A civilization's replacement for a standard building, with an edge.
- *Unique unit*: A civilization's replacement for a standard unit, with an edge.
- *United Nations*: World project unlocked by Mass Media, hosting votes on resolutions and diplomatic victory.
- *Universal Suffrage*: Government civic that lets cities buy production with gold and adds +1 hammer to towns.

== V

- *Vassal*: A civilization under another's protection, sharing part of its research and gold.
- *Vassalage*: Legal civic granting new units +2 experience and extra free units.
- *Village*: The third growth stage of a cottage.

== W

- *War elephant*: Mounted unit requiring ivory, powerful in the classical era.
- *Watermill*: Riverside improvement providing food and production.
- *Windmill*: Hill improvement providing commerce, and food in later eras.
- *Workshop*: Improvement trading food for production.
- *Writing*: Technology unlocking libraries and scholarship.

== Z

- *Ziggurat*: Sumerian unique building replacing the Courthouse.
